\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Let $P_\alpha=-\Delta_g+\langle\nabla\alpha,\nabla\cdot\rangle$ be a Witten operator on a closed connected Riemannian manifold, acting in $L^2(e^{-\alpha}\,d\mathrm{vol}_g)$. The spectrum of $P_\alpha$ does not determine $(g,\alpha)$. The full multiplication table of eigenfunctions does. This paper isolates a finite piece of that table.

Fix cutoffs $\Lambda\le\Lambda'<\infty$ and let $\mathcal{V}$ be the sum of eigenspaces of eigenvalue at most $\Lambda$. The visible packet is the restriction of the triple products $\Phi$ to pairs from $\mathcal{V}$ against modes up to $\Lambda'$, together with the weighted tail
$$R(u,v)=-P(I-\Pi_{\Lambda'})(uv)=-\sum_{\lambda>\Lambda'}\lambda\,\Phi(u,v,e_\lambda)\,e_\lambda$$
and its uniform size $\Delta=\sup\lVert R(u,v)\rVert_2$ over unit $u,v\in\mathcal{V}$. Write $t=(I-\Pi_{\Lambda'})(uv)$. No Weyl law, no potential, and no curvature bound is used. A Wyman bound on window triples guarantees that the coefficients of $R$ exist.

If $R=0$, the packet determines $(X,g,\alpha)$ up to a $C^\infty$ isometry of $g$ and an additive constant on $\alpha$. If $\lVert t\rVert_{C^1}$ is smaller than a threshold depending on the embedding of $X$ in $\mathcal{V}^*$, evaluation realises $X$ as a $C^1$ submanifold $\Sigma\subset\mathcal{V}^*$ on which
$$\lVert\iota^*g_\Sigma-g\rVert_{C^k}+\lVert\alpha_\Sigma\circ\iota-\alpha-c\rVert_{C^k}\le C(d,k)\,\lVert R\rVert_{C^k(\Sigma)},$$
with $d=\dim\mathcal{V}$. That bound is not a statement about $\Delta$. When the tail of $\Phi$ occupies a finite slot, Sobolev embedding on $\Sigma$ converts $\lVert R\rVert_{C^k}$ into a multiple of $\Delta$, with constants depending only on window dimensions and $k$.

If in addition $\alpha=0$, an exact packet realises $X$ as a union of orbits of a compact Lie group of isometries of the reconstructed metric (Theorem 21). When $\dim\mathcal{V}=n+1$ and the quadratic $Q=\sum u_a^2$ is constant, that action is transitive and $X$ is isometric to a sphere of radius $\sqrt Q$.

The unweighted first window on the sphere is the minimal finite-tail case: $\Lambda=n$, $\dim\mathcal{V}=n+1$, and $R=0$ already at $\Lambda'=2n+2$. That frame recognizes the unit round sphere exactly, and recognizes it with linear $C^0$ stability for an isolated cluster of first $n+1$ harmonics with an almost spherical table. Those statements are frame-side. They are not Obata theorems.

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} sphere, homogeneous space, spectral window, triple products, Witten Operators, eigenfunctions, inverse problem
\end{abstract}

\tableofcontents

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

A Witten operator on a closed Riemannian manifold $(X,g)$ is the weighted Laplacian
$$P_\alpha=-\Delta_g+\langle\nabla\alpha,\nabla\cdot\rangle$$
acting in $L^2(e^{-\alpha}\,d\mathrm{vol}_g)$ \cite{Wit82}. Its spectrum is discrete, nonnegative, and unbounded, with kernel the constant functions. The geometric data of the pair are the metric $g$ and the weight $\alpha$; equivalently, Riemannian volume and the measure $\mu=e^{-\alpha}\,d\mathrm{vol}_g$. Ordinary Laplace–Beltrami operators are the case $\alpha=0$.

The spectrum alone does not determine $(g,\alpha)$. What does determine them, in the large, is the multiplication table of eigenfunctions
$$\Phi(u,v,w)=\int_X uvw\,d\mu$$
together with the list of eigenvalues \cite{Sch24,Sch26}. That statement consumes every eigenspace. The purpose of this article is a finite substitute.
Fix cutoffs $\Lambda\le\Lambda'<\infty$ and let
$$\mathcal{V}=\bigoplus_{\lambda\le\Lambda}\ker(P-\lambda)$$
be the corresponding spectral window. Products of window functions need not lie in $\mathcal{V}$. Write $m(u,v)=\Pi_{\Lambda'}(uv)$ for the projection onto modes up to $\Lambda'$, and
$$R(u,v)=-P\bigl(I-\Pi_{\Lambda'}\bigr)(uv)
=-\sum_{\lambda>\Lambda'}\lambda\,\Phi(u,v,e_\lambda)\,e_\lambda$$
for the weighted tail of those products. Throughout, $u,v\in\mathcal{V}$. The uniform size of the tail is
$$\Delta=\sup_{\lVert u\rVert_2=\lVert v\rVert_2=1}\lVert R(u,v)\rVert_2.$$

The exact subclass is $R=0$ at a finite $\Lambda'$, which is an algebraic constraint on the window, not an asymptotic one. A Wyman bound on window triples guarantees that the coefficients of $R$ exist \cite{Wym}.

The visible packet is
$$\bigl(\Lambda,\ \Lambda',\ \sigma(P)\cap[0,\Lambda'],\ \Phi\big|_{\mathcal{V}\otimes\mathcal{V}\otimes\mathcal{V}'},\ R\bigr).$$

The cometric $\Gamma$ of $g$ is not an extra input. The identity
$$P(uv)=u\,Pv+v\,Pu-2\Gamma(u,v)$$
computes $\Gamma$ on $\mathcal{V}$ from $(P,\Phi)$ with remainder $R$. Two nondegeneracy hypotheses close the packet: evaluation $X\to\mathcal{V}^\ast$ is a smooth embedding, and $\{du:u\in\mathcal{V}\}$ spans $T^*X$. No Weyl law is assumed, no potential is present, and no curvature bound is used.

If $R=0$, the packet determines $(X,g,\alpha)$ up to a $C^\infty$ isometry of $g$ and an additive constant on $\alpha$. If $\alpha=0$, that pair carries a compact Lie group of isometries whose orbits decompose $X$ (§9); if also $\dim\mathcal{V}=n+1$ and $Q$ is constant, $X$ is a sphere. If $\lVert t\rVert_{C^1}$ is smaller than a threshold depending on the embedding of $X$ in $\mathcal{V}^*$, evaluation realises $X$ as a $C^1$ submanifold $\Sigma\subset\mathcal{V}^*$ on which
$$\lVert\iota^*g_\Sigma-g\rVert_{C^k}+\lVert\alpha_\Sigma\circ\iota-\alpha-c\rVert_{C^k}\le C(d,k)\,\lVert R\rVert_{C^k(\Sigma)},$$
with $d=\dim\mathcal{V}$. When the tail of $\Phi$ occupies a finite slot, Sobolev embedding on $\Sigma$ converts this to a linear bound in $\Delta$ whose constants depend only on window dimensions and $k$. In both regimes the manifold is recovered as the space of multiplicative characters of the window, the metric by dualizing $\Gamma$, and the weight from the ratio of $\mu$ to Riemannian volume.

The theorem is recognition, not construction: $P$ is given as Witten, and the packet is asked to recover the geometry that produced it. It is not a spectral uniqueness theorem. Two Witten operators with the same spectrum, or even the same $\sigma(P)\cap[0,\Lambda']$, need not be isometric; the missing data are $\Phi$ and $R$. Two operators with the same exact packet ($R=0$) are isometric, with $\alpha$ agreeing up to a constant. Two operators with the same approximate packet need not be isometric; they are $C^k$-close to isometric on the scale of $\lVert R\rVert_{C^k(\Sigma)}$.

A distinguished slice is the unweighted first window on the sphere.
There one may take \(\Lambda=n\), \(\dim\mathcal{V}=n+1\), and \(R=0\)
already at \(\Lambda'=2n+2\): products of linear coordinates on \(S^n\)
occupy only the constant slot and the quadratic harmonics. Theorem 3
recognizes the unit round sphere from that exact frame. Theorem 4 is
linear \(C^0\) stability for an isolated cluster of the first \(n+1\)
harmonics whose visible products lie in \(\eta\)-gaps about \(0\) and
\(2n+2\); on a sufficiently small \(C^2\) ball about the round metric
those gaps persist and the table deficits are \(O\bigl(\|g-g_{\mathrm{rd}}\|_{C^2}\bigr)\).
Those two statements are compared with Obata’s theorem \cite{Oba62} and with
quantitative Obata theorems on the Ricci side \cite{CMS}. They are not
Ricci theorems. They use a linear frame and its products, not a lower
bound on Ricci curvature and a single first eigenfunction.

If \(\alpha=0\) and the window extends to an exact, finite Parseval tower with
constant slot quadratics, evaluation is a homothety into a product of
spheres with parallel second fundamental form, and \(X\) is an orbit
of an \(s\)-representation (Theorem 32).

The article is organized as follows. Section 2 fixes the Witten category and the window packet. Section 3 extracts $\Gamma$ from the multiplication rule with remainder $R$. Section 4 identifies points of $X$ with characters of the window. Section 5 dualizes $\Gamma$ to a metric and reads $\alpha$ from the two measures. Section 6 assembles Theorems 1 and 2. Section 7 specializes to the unweighted first window on the sphere. Section 8 records what the packet does not determine. Section 9 shows that an exact unweighted window is a union of orbits of a compact linear group of isometries, and a sphere when $d=n+1$ and $Q$ is constant.

Geometry sits in the multiplication table, not in the list of eigenvalues. The contribution here is that a finite, explicitly tailed piece of that table already reconstructs a Witten pair, and that the unit sphere is the minimal unweighted instance of the same mechanism; an exact unweighted window is homogeneous once it has an open orbit.

\subsection{Results}
Standing hypotheses: $X$ closed and connected, $P=P_\alpha$ a Witten operator \cite{Wit82}, packet as in §2, $u,v\in\mathcal{V}$, Wyman on window triples \cite{Wym}, W1, W2.

\begin{theorem}[exact recognition]
\label{thm-1}
Assume $R=0$. The packet
$$\bigl(\Lambda,\Lambda',\sigma(P)\cap[0,\Lambda'],\Phi\big|_{\mathcal{V}\otimes\mathcal{V}\otimes\mathcal{V}'}\bigr)$$
determines $(X,g,\alpha)$ up to a $C^\infty$ Riemannian isometry of $g$ and an additive constant on $\alpha$. Evaluation is a Riemannian isometry of $(X,g)$ onto the character space of $(\mathcal{V},m)$ in $\mathcal{V}^\ast$, and $P$ is the Witten operator of the reconstructed pair.

If in addition $\alpha=0$, then $X$ is a union of orbits of a compact Lie group of isometries of the reconstructed metric (Theorem 21). If also $\dim\mathcal{V}=n+1$ and $Q$ is constant, $X$ is a sphere.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[approximate recognition]
\label{thm-2}
Let $d=\dim\mathcal{V}$.
\begin{description}
\item[(i)] There exists $\tau_*=\tau_*(d,\iota(X))>0$ such that if
$$\sup_{\lVert u\rVert_2=\lVert v\rVert_2=1}\lVert t(u,v)\rVert_{C^1(X)}<\tau_*,$$
then evaluation realises $X$ as a $C^1$ embedded submanifold $\Sigma\subset\mathcal{V}^*$ and
$$\lVert\iota^*g_\Sigma-g\rVert_{C^k}+\lVert\alpha_\Sigma\circ\iota-\alpha-c\rVert_{C^k}
\le C(d,k)\,\lVert R\rVert_{C^k(\Sigma)}$$
for every $k\ge 0$, with $c$ an additive constant on $\alpha$. The diffeomorphism $\iota$ is $C^\infty$ if $t=0$. The smallness parameter is $\lVert t\rVert_{C^1}$, equivalently $\lVert R\rVert_{C^k}$ after Lemma 9(i); it is not $\Delta$.

\item[(ii)] If the tail that defines $R$ occupies a finite slot of dimension $d_{\mathrm{tail}}$ (or is cut at a finite $\Lambda''$), Lemma 9(ii) supplies $\Delta_*=\Delta_*(d,d_{\mathrm{tail}},\iota(X))>0$ such that $\Delta<\Delta_*$ implies the conclusions of (i), and
$$\lVert R\rVert_{C^k(\Sigma)}\le C(d,d_{\mathrm{tail}},k)\,\Delta.$$
In that case $\iota$ is a $C^k$ almost-isometry on the scale of $\Delta$.
\end{description}
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[round sphere, exact window]
\label{thm-a}
Assume $\alpha=0$, $\dim\mathcal{V}=n+1$, $\Lambda=n$, and $R=0$ at $\Lambda'=2n+2$. Suppose there is a frame $x_0,\dots,x_n$ in $\mathcal{V}$ with $Px_a=nx_a$, $\int x_ax_b=\delta_{ab}$, $\int x_a=0$, $\sum_ax_a^2=1$, each $x_ax_b-\delta_{ab}/(n+1)$ an eigenfunction at $2n+2$, and $\{dx_a\}$ spanning $T^*X$. Then $X$ is isometric to the unit round sphere, $P=-\Delta$, and $\operatorname{span}\{x_a\}=H_1$. Scaling: the pair $(\lambda,2\lambda+2\lambda/n)$ gives radius $\sqrt{n/\lambda}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[round sphere, quantitative cluster]
\label{thm-b}
Let \(\lambda_1\le\cdots\le\lambda_{n+1}\) be the first \(n+1\) positive
eigenvalues of \(P\) counted with multiplicity, and let
\(\mathcal{V}=\operatorname{span}\{x_1,\dots,x_{n+1}\}\) be the joint
span of corresponding \(L^2\)-orthonormal eigenfunctions. Write
\[
w=\lambda_{n+1}-\lambda_1,\qquad
\bar\lambda=\frac1{n+1}\sum_{i=1}^{n+1}\lambda_i,
\]
and assume the cluster is isolated:
\[
w<\lambda_{n+2}-\lambda_{n+1}.
\]
Fix \(\eta\in\bigl(0,\min\{n,n+4\}/2\bigr)\) smaller than the first two
spherical gaps, and a cutoff \(\Lambda'\ge 2n+2+\eta\). Set
\[
\Pi_{\mathrm{sph}}^\eta
=\Pi_{[0,\eta]}+\Pi_{[2n+2-\eta,\,2n+2+\eta]}.
\]
Allow the table deficits
\begin{align*}
\delta_w&=w+\lvert\bar\lambda-n\rvert,\\
\delta_{\mathrm{on}}
&=\sum_{a,b}\Bigl\lvert\int x_ax_b-\delta_{ab}\Bigr\rvert
+\sum_a\Bigl\lvert\int x_a\Bigr\rvert,\\
\delta_1&=\bigl\|\textstyle\sum_a x_a^2-1\bigr\|_{C^0},\\
\delta_\Phi
&=\sup_{a,b}\bigl\|(I-\Pi_{\mathrm{sph}}^\eta)\,\Pi_{\Lambda'}(x_ax_b)\bigr\|_{L^2},\\
\Delta_{\mathrm{fr}}
&=\sup_{a,b}\lVert R_{\Lambda'}(x_a,x_b)\rVert_2,\\
\delta_{\mathrm{rank}}
&=\text{rank gap of }\Gamma(x_a,x_b).
\end{align*}
Set
\[
\delta=\delta_w+\delta_{\mathrm{on}}+\delta_1+\delta_\Phi
+\Delta_{\mathrm{fr}}+\delta_{\mathrm{rank}}.
\]
If \(\delta<\delta_*(n,\eta)\), then \(X\) is a \(C^1\)-graph over \(S^n\)
and
\[
d_{\mathrm{GH}}(X,S^n)+d_{C^0}(g,g_{\mathrm{rd}})
\le C(n,\eta,\Lambda')\,\delta.
\]
If \(\Lambda'=2n+2+\eta\), the constant depends only on \(n\) and \(\eta\).
This is linear stability of the first \(n+1\) harmonics of Theorem 3,
allowing the first slot to split and the quadratic slot to drift inside
an \(\eta\)-gap. It does not use Ricci curvature [Oba62; CMS23].
When \(w=0\), \(\eta=0^+\), and \(\Lambda'=2n+2\), it specialises to
stability of a single eigenspace with exact projectors
\(\Pi_0+\Pi_{2n+2}\).
\end{theorem}
On a sufficiently small \(C^2\) ball about \(g_{\mathrm{rd}}\) the gaps
about \(0\), \(n\), and \(2n+2\) persist, and
\[
\delta\le C(n,\eta)\,\|g-g_{\mathrm{rd}}\|_{C^2}.
\]
Thus Theorem 4 applies on that ball. It does not apply to a residual
set in \(\mathcal{M}\): a generic perturbation may break isolation or
spray products through the \(\eta\)-gaps.

The \(C^0\) bound and min-max give
\(\lvert\lambda_k(P)-\lambda_k(S^n)\rvert\le C(n,k,\eta)\,\delta\)
for each fixed \(k\). Tanno’s uniqueness is the converse neighbourhood
statement with \(\operatorname{Spec}(P)=\operatorname{Spec}(S^n)\);
the paper does not assume the spectra coincide.

\section{The Packet}
Let $X$ be a closed connected smooth manifold and let
$$P=P_\alpha=-\Delta_g+\langle\nabla\alpha,\nabla\cdot\rangle$$
act in $L^2(X,\mu)$ with $d\mu=e^{-\alpha}\,d\mathrm{vol}_g$ \cite{Wit82}. Assume $P$ is essentially self-adjoint, with discrete spectrum
$$0=\lambda_0<\lambda_1\le\lambda_2\le\cdots\to+\infty$$
and $\ker P=\operatorname{span}\{1\}$. The principal symbol of $P$ is the cometric $\Gamma$ of $g$. For all smooth $u,v$,

\begin{equation}
P(uv)=u\,Pv+v\,Pu-2\Gamma(u,v).\tag{A1}
\end{equation}

Fix cutoffs $\Lambda\le\Lambda'<\infty$. Write
$$\mathcal{V}=\bigoplus_{\lambda\le\Lambda}\ker(P-\lambda),\qquad
\mathcal{V}'=\bigoplus_{\lambda\le\Lambda'}\ker(P-\lambda),$$
and let $\Pi_{\Lambda'}$ be the orthogonal projection onto $\mathcal{V}'$. Throughout, $u,v\in\mathcal{V}$. Set
$$m(u,v)=\Pi_{\Lambda'}(uv),\qquad
\Phi(u,v,w)=\int_X uvw\,d\mu.$$

The weighted tail of the product is
$$R(u,v)=-P\bigl(I-\Pi_{\Lambda'}\bigr)(uv)
=-\sum_{\lambda>\Lambda'}\lambda\,\Phi(u,v,e_\lambda)\,e_\lambda,$$
and
$$\Delta=\sup\bigl\{\lVert R(u,v)\rVert_2:\ u,v\in\mathcal{V},\ \lVert u\rVert_2=\lVert v\rVert_2=1\bigr\}.$$

The exact subclass is $R=0$, equivalently $\Delta=0$.

\textbf{Wyman}. For $u,v\in\mathcal{V}$ and an $L^2$-normalized eigenfunction $e_\lambda$,
$$\sum_{\lambda}\bigl\lvert\Phi(u,v,e_\lambda)\bigr\rvert^2$$
is controlled by the measure of frequency triangles with side lengths comparable to $(\sqrt{\lambda_u},\sqrt{\lambda_v},\sqrt{\lambda})$ \cite{Wym}. In particular there is rapid decay of the $\ell^2$ mass of $\Phi(u,v,\,\cdot\,)$ in the classically forbidden region
$$\sqrt{\lambda}\ge(1+\varepsilon)\bigl(\sqrt{\lambda_u}+\sqrt{\lambda_v}\bigr),$$
for every $\varepsilon>0$. The paper uses only the following crude consequence: the coefficients of $R$ exist, and if a finite slot above $\Lambda'$ is fixed, they are bounded by a constant depending on that slot, on $\Lambda$, and on $\lVert u\rVert_2\lVert v\rVert_2$.
A pointwise bound
$$\bigl\lvert\Phi(u,v,e_\lambda)\bigr\rvert
\le C\,(1+\lambda_u+\lambda_v+\lambda)^\delta\,\lVert u\rVert_2\lVert v\rVert_2$$
is not claimed in \cite{Wym}; when it is invoked below it is as a convenient majorant on a finite slot, not as Wyman’s theorem.

\textbf{W1}. Evaluation $\iota:X\to\mathcal{V}^\ast$, $\iota(x)(u)=u(x)$, is a smooth embedding onto a closed submanifold of $\mathcal{V}^\ast$. The same holds for evaluation $X\to(\mathcal{V}')^\ast$. Characters are therefore evaluations on $\mathcal{V}'$, and the comparison error $E$ of §4 vanishes.


\textbf{W2}. The differentials $\{du:u\in\mathcal{V}\}$ span $T^*X$ at every point.

The visible packet is
$$\bigl(\Lambda,\ \Lambda',\ \sigma(P)\cap[0,\Lambda'],\ \Phi\big|_{\mathcal{V}\otimes\mathcal{V}\otimes\mathcal{V}'},\ R\bigr).$$
On pairs from $\mathcal{V}$, (A1) reads
$$2\Gamma(u,v)=u\,Pv+v\,Pu-P\bigl(m(u,v)\bigr)+R(u,v).$$
Thus $\Gamma$ on $\mathcal{V}$ is not an independent input. No Weyl law, no potential, and no curvature bound is assumed. Dimension of $X$ is the rank in W2.

\section{The Cometric From the Window}

\begin{lemma}
\label{lma-1}
Let \(u,v\in\mathcal{V}\). Split the pointwise product as
\[
uv=m(u,v)+t(u,v),\qquad
m(u,v)=\Pi_{\Lambda'}(uv),\qquad
t(u,v)=(I-\Pi_{\Lambda'})(uv).
\]
The visible product \(m(u,v)\) is assembled from the in-window table:
\[
m(u,v)=\sum_{\lambda\le\Lambda'}\Phi(u,v,e_\lambda)\,e_\lambda.
\]
Identity (A1) then reads
\[
2\Gamma(u,v)=u\,Pv+v\,Pu-P\bigl(m(u,v)\bigr)+R(u,v),
\]
where
\[
R(u,v)=-P\,t(u,v)=-\sum_{\lambda>\Lambda'}\lambda\,\Phi(u,v,e_\lambda)\,e_\lambda.
\]
Thus \(\Gamma\) on \(\mathcal{V}\) is determined by \(P|_{\mathcal{V}}\), by \(\Phi\) on
\(\mathcal{V}\otimes\mathcal{V}\otimes\mathcal{V}'\), and by the tail \(R\).
In particular \(\lVert R(u,v)\rVert_2\le\Delta\lVert u\rVert_2\lVert v\rVert_2\).
If \(R=0\), then \(t=0\) and \(\Gamma(u,v)\) is determined by \((P,\Phi)\).
The bilinear map \(\Gamma:\mathcal{V}\times\mathcal{V}\to C^\infty(X)\) does not
depend on the orthonormal bases used to expand \(\Phi\).

On the first spherical window one may further split
\[
m(x_a,x_b)=\Pi_{\mathrm{sph}}(x_ax_b)+(I-\Pi_{\mathrm{sph}})(x_ax_b),
\]
with \(\Pi_{\mathrm{sph}}=\Pi_0+\Pi_{2n+2}\). The first summand is the model
table of Theorem 3; the second is the in-window table error measured by
\(\delta_\Phi\) in Theorem 4. Lemma 5 converts
\(\delta_w+\delta_\Phi+\Delta_{\mathrm{fr}}\) into a \(C^0\) error on
\(\Gamma(x_a,x_b)\) relative to \(\delta_{ab}-x_ax_b\).

\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Identity (A1) is \(2\Gamma(u,v)=u\,Pv+v\,Pu-P(uv)\). Substitute
\(uv=m+t\) and set \(R=-Pt\). The expansion of \(m\) is the definition of
\(\Phi\) against \(\mathcal{V}'\). If \(R=0\), then \(t=0\), so \(m(u,v)=uv\)
and every coefficient of \(uv\) against \(\mathcal{V}'\) is a value of \(\Phi\).
Basis-independence: \(P\) and \(\Phi\) are defined on \(\mathcal{V}\) without
choices, and \(m\) is orthogonal projection of the pointwise product.

On a frame of \(n+1\) eigenfunctions with eigenvalues \(\lambda_a\),
identity (A1) reads
\[
2\Gamma(x_a,x_b)=(\lambda_a+\lambda_b)x_ax_b-P\bigl(m(x_a,x_b)\bigr)+R_{\Lambda'}.
\]
Write \(\lambda_a=n+\varepsilon_a\) with \(\lvert\varepsilon_a\rvert\le\delta_w\).
Split \(m=\Pi_{\mathrm{sph}}m+(I-\Pi_{\mathrm{sph}})m\). The model
computation of Theorem 3 gives
\[
P\bigl(\Pi_{\mathrm{sph}}(x_ax_b)\bigr)
=(2n+2)\Bigl(x_ax_b-\frac{\delta_{ab}}{n+1}\Bigr)+E_{\mathrm{sph}},
\]
with \(\lVert E_{\mathrm{sph}}\rVert_{L^2}\le C(n)\delta_w\). The
remainder \((I-\Pi_{\mathrm{sph}})m\) is the in-window table error
measured by \(\delta_\Phi\), and \(R_{\Lambda'}\) is measured by
\(\Delta_{\mathrm{fr}}\). Hence
\[
\bigl\lVert\Gamma(x_a,x_b)-(\delta_{ab}-x_ax_b)\bigr\rVert_{L^2}
\le C(n)\bigl(\delta_w+\delta_\Phi+\Delta_{\mathrm{fr}}\bigr),
\]
which is the cometric input to Theorem 4.

The deficit \(\delta_\Phi\) is the \(L^2\) mass of \(\Pi_{\Lambda'}(x_ax_b)\) off the
cluster projector
\[
\Pi_{\mathrm{sph}}^\eta=\Pi_{[0,\eta]}+\Pi_{[2n+2-\eta,\,2n+2+\eta]}.
\]
It is not the tail \(\Delta_{\mathrm{fr}}\), which lives above \(\Lambda'\).

Write \(\Delta_g\) as a divergence-form operator on the fixed space
\(L^2(d\mathrm{vol}_{g_{\mathrm{rd}}})\). On a sufficiently small \(C^2\)
ball about \(g_{\mathrm{rd}}\), the resolvent is close in operator norm
and the contours about \(0\), \(n\), and \(2n+2\) miss
\(\operatorname{Spec}(\Delta_g)\) \cite{Kato, Ch.~IV}. Thus
\(\Pi_{\mathrm{sph}}^\eta\) has rank bounded in terms of \(n\) only, the
first \(n+1\) modes remain an isolated cluster, and
\[
\delta\le C(n,\eta)\,\|g-g_{\mathrm{rd}}\|_{C^2}.
\]
Theorem 4 therefore applies on that ball in \(\mathcal{M}^2(M)\) for
\(M\simeq S^n\). Individual eigenvalues in the cluster may split; only
the joint span \(\mathcal{V}\) is used.

The specialisation \(w=0\), \(\eta\to 0\), \(\Lambda'=2n+2+\eta\) recovers
exact projectors \(\Pi_0+\Pi_{2n+2}\) and the single-eigenspace stability
of Theorem 3.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Sogge and $\lVert t\rVert_{C^0}$]
Hörmander–Sogge gives $\lVert e_\lambda\rVert_{C^0}\le C_S(n)\,(1+\lambda)^{(n-1)/4}$ \cite{Sog88}. Expanding $t$ and applying Cauchy–Schwarz with the weight in $R=Pt$ yields, for a tail supported in a finite slot $\Lambda<\lambda\le\Lambda''$,
$$\lVert t\rVert_{C^0}\le C(n,\Lambda',\Lambda'')\,\Delta,$$
and therefore
$$\lVert R\rVert_{C^0(X)}\le C(n,\Lambda',\Lambda'')\,\Delta.$$
Wyman bounds the coefficients of $R$ \cite{Wym}; Sogge bounds the modes. Neither is a curvature comparison.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
 If the $\Phi$-tail that defines $R$ occupies a finite slot of dimension $d_{\mathrm{tail}}$, all norms on that slot are equivalent and
$$\lVert R\rVert_{C^k(X)}\le C(d,d_{\mathrm{tail}},k)\,\Delta.$$
If the tail is infinite, $\lVert R\rVert_{C^k}$ remains the modulus.
\end{remark}

\begin{corollary}
 If $R=0$, the Gram functions $\Gamma(u,v)$ for $u,v\in\mathcal{V}$ are packet data. W2 is then a nondegeneracy condition on those functions. If $\Delta>0$, the same Gram functions are determined up to an $L^2$ error of size $\Delta$, and up to a $C^k$ error of size $\lVert R\rVert_{C^k}$ after the comparison of §4.
\end{corollary}

\section{Characters}
Let $m:\mathcal{V}\times\mathcal{V}\to\mathcal{V}'$ be the packet product of §2. By W1, evaluation
$$\iota:X\to\mathcal{V}^\ast,\qquad
\iota':X\to(\mathcal{V}')^\ast$$
are smooth embeddings onto closed submanifolds. Characters of the window are evaluations on $\mathcal{V}'$. In particular the comparison error between $R$ as a function on $X$ and $R$ as a function on the image $\Sigma=\iota(X)$ vanishes: $R_\Sigma\circ\iota=R_X$.

\begin{lemma}
\label{lma-2}
Let $d=\dim\mathcal{V}$ and let $\iota:X\to\mathcal{V}^*$ be the evaluation of W1. Write $t(u,v)=(I-\Pi_{\Lambda'})(uv)$ for $u,v\in\mathcal{V}$.

\begin{description}
\item[(i)] (infinite tail) There exists $\tau_*=\tau_*(d,\iota(X))>0$ such that if
$$\sup_{\lVert u\rVert_2=\lVert v\rVert_2=1}\lVert t(u,v)\rVert_{C^1(X)}<\tau_*,$$
then $\iota(X)$ is a $C^\infty$ embedded submanifold $\Sigma\subset\mathcal{V}^*$, $\iota:X\to\Sigma$ is a $C^1$ diffeomorphism (and $C^\infty$ if $t=0$), and for every $k\ge 0$
$$c(k)^{-1}\,\lVert R_X\rVert_{C^k(X)}
\le\lVert R_\Sigma\rVert_{C^k(\Sigma)}
\le c(k)\,\lVert R_X\rVert_{C^k(X)},$$
with $c(k)=C(d,k)$. The smallness parameter here is $\lVert t\rVert_{C^1}$, not $\Delta$.

\item[(ii)] (finite slot) If the $\Phi$-tail that defines $R$ occupies a finite slot of dimension $d_{\mathrm{tail}}$ (or is cut at a finite $\Lambda''$), then Remarks 6 and 7 give
$$\lVert t\rVert_{C^1}\le C(d,d_{\mathrm{tail}},n,\Lambda',\Lambda'')\,\Delta.$$
Hence there exists $\Delta_*=\Delta_*(d,d_{\mathrm{tail}},\iota(X))>0$ such that $\Delta<\Delta_*$ implies the conclusions of (i), and
$$\lVert R\rVert_{C^k(\Sigma)}\le C(d,d_{\mathrm{tail}},k)\,\Delta.$$
\end{description}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
W1 already makes $\iota$ a smooth embedding onto a closed submanifold, so the first claim of (i) does not use the size of $t$. The product map $F(\xi;u,v)=\xi(m(u,v))-\xi(u)\xi(v)$ vanishes on $\iota(X)$ up to $t$. Evaluation extends to $\mathcal{V}'$, so at $\xi=\iota(x)$ one has $\xi(m(u,v))=m(u,v)(x)$ and the only defect is $t(x)$. Differentiating $F$ in the $\mathcal{V}^*$-directions at $\iota(x)$ yields the family $(u,v)\mapsto\xi(u)\,dv+\xi(v)\,du$ on $T_x^*X$. W2 says $\{du:u\in\mathcal{V}\}$ spans $T_x^*X$, so this family is transverse to $\iota(X)$. If $\lVert t\rVert_{C^1}<\tau_*$, $F$ is a $C^1$-small perturbation of a section that vanishes exactly on $\iota(X)$. Persistence of transverse embeddings keeps $\iota$ a $C^1$ diffeomorphism onto its image. If $t=0$, then $R=0$, $F$ vanishes exactly on $\iota(X)$, and $\iota$ is $C^\infty$. The $C^k$ comparison of $R_X$ and $R_\Sigma$ is the chain rule for $\iota$ and $\iota^{-1}$, whose $C^{k-1}$ norms stay bounded for $\lVert t\rVert_{C^1}<\tau_*$.

For (ii), a finite slot makes every $C^k$ norm on the tail equivalent to the $L^2$ norm of $R=-Pt$. Remark 6 supplies the $C^0$ bound on $t$ via Sogge; equivalence of norms on a space of dimension $d_{\mathrm{tail}}$ upgrades that to $C^1$. Thus $\Delta$ small implies $\lVert t\rVert_{C^1}$ small, and (i) applies.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[weighted tails] The hypothesis of Lemma 9(i) is $\lVert t\rVert_{C^1}<\tau_*$. On a finite slot this follows from $\Delta$ by Remarks 6 and 7. On an infinite tail it does not, and the paper does not claim that it does. The same Sogge bound that gives Remark 6 still converts a weighted norm of $\Phi$ into $\lVert t\rVert_{C^1}$. Expanding
$$t(u,v)=\sum_{\lambda>\Lambda'}\Phi(u,v,e_\lambda)\,e_\lambda$$
and using $\lVert e_\lambda\rVert_{C^0}\le C_S(n)(1+\lambda)^{(n-1)/4}$ together with $\lVert\nabla e_\lambda\rVert_{C^0}\le C(n)(1+\lambda)^{(n-1)/4+1/2}$ yields
$$\lVert t\rVert_{C^1}
\le C(n)\sum_{\lambda>\Lambda'}(1+\lambda)^{\frac{n-1}{4}+\frac12}\lvert\Phi(u,v,e_\lambda)\rvert.$$
Cauchy–Schwarz against the same weights gives the $\ell^2$ form
$$\lVert t\rVert_{C^1}
\le C(n)\Bigl(\sum_{\lambda>\Lambda'}(1+\lambda)^{\frac{n-1}{2}+1}\lvert\Phi(u,v,e_\lambda)\rvert^2\Bigr)^{1/2}.$$
Either right-hand side may be written $\Delta_{C^1}(u,v)$. Then Lemma 9(i) applies as soon as $\sup\Delta_{C^1}<\tau_*/C(n)$. This $\Delta_{C^1}$ is not the packet scalar $\Delta=\sup\lVert R\rVert_2$. The two coincide, up to a constant depending on the slot, only when the sum is finite, which is Lemma 9(ii). Wyman controls the unweighted $\ell^2$ mass of $\Phi$ off the frequency triangle; it does not by itself bound $\Delta_{C^1}$ on an infinite tail. If a uniform scalar is wanted in that regime, $\Delta_{C^1}$ (or a cut at a finite $\Lambda''$, with the far tail absorbed into $R$) is the quantity that should be listed with the packet.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark} Theorem 2’s general bound is (i), written in $\lVert R\rVert_{C^k(\Sigma)}$. The conversion to a multiple of $\Delta$ is (ii). Theorem 4 uses only (ii), with $d=n+1$ and a finite cluster about the slot $2n+2$.
\end{remark}

\begin{corollary}
If $R=0$, $X$ is identified with the character space of the finite algebra $(\mathcal{V},m)$ as a $C^\infty$ embedded submanifold of $\mathcal{V}^\ast$. If $\Delta<\Delta_*$, the same identification holds as a $C^1$ diffeomorphism, and $\lVert R\rVert_{C^k(\Sigma)}$ may be used interchangeably with $\lVert R\rVert_{C^k(X)}$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{remark}
Residual ambiguity is an orthogonal change of basis of $\mathcal{V}$ preserving $\Phi$, a compact subgroup of $O(d)$. The threshold $\Delta_*$ depends on $d$ and on the $C^1$ geometry of $\iota(X)$ at $\Delta=0$. It is not universal in $n$ except on the spherical window of §7.
\end{remark}

\section{Metric and Weight}
\begin{lemma}Assume Lemma~\ref{lma-1} and W2. There is a unique Riemannian metric $g$ on $X$ such that
$$\Gamma(u,v)=g^{-1}(du,dv)\qquad\text{for all }u,v\in\mathcal{V}.$$
This $g$ is the metric whose cometric is the principal symbol of $P$. After Lemma 9,
$$\bigl\lVert\iota^*g_\Sigma-g\bigr\rVert_{C^k}
\le C(d,k)\,\lVert R\rVert_{C^k(\Sigma)}.$$
If the tail of $\Phi$ occupies a finite slot of dimension $d_{\mathrm{tail}}$, the right-hand side is at most $C(d,d_{\mathrm{tail}},k)\,\Delta$.
\label{lma-3}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
W2 says $\{du:u\in\mathcal{V}\}$ spans $T^*X$. The assignment $(du,dv)\mapsto\Gamma(u,v)$ is well-defined on that spanning set: $\Gamma(u,u)=\sigma_P(du,du)$, so $du=0$ implies $\Gamma(u,\cdot)=0$. Positive-definiteness is the assumption that the principal symbol of $P$ is an inner product. Dualizing gives $g$. In a local frame $\{u_a\}\subset\mathcal{V}$ of rank $n=\dim X$,
$$g^{ab}=\Gamma(u_a,u_b)$$
on $\operatorname{span}\{du_a\}$, and $g$ is the inverse matrix. Independence of the frame is change of basis for a cometric. The $C^k$ estimate is Lemma 5 plus the comparison of $\lVert R\rVert_{C^k}$ on $X$ and on $\Sigma$ from Lemma 9. Finite-tail conversion is the remark in §3.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}
Let $g$ be the metric of Lemma 14 and let $\mu$ be the measure determined by
$$\int uv\,d\mu=\Phi(1,u,v),\qquad u,v\in\mathcal{V},$$
extended by continuity to $L^2(\mu)$. Then $\mu$ is a smooth positive multiple of $\mathrm{vol}_g$. The function
$$\alpha=-\log\frac{d\mu}{d\mathrm{vol}_g}$$
is smooth, unique up to an additive constant, and the given operator $P$ is the Witten operator of $(g,\alpha)$. If $\Delta<\Delta_*$,
$$\bigl\lVert\alpha_\Sigma\circ\iota-\alpha-c\bigr\rVert_{C^k}
\le C(d,k)\,\lVert R\rVert_{C^k(\Sigma)}.$$
\label{lma-4}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
By the standing category, $P$ is already the Witten operator of some pair $(g_0,\alpha_0)$. Lemma 14 identifies $g=g_0$, because both metrics have the same principal symbol. The inner product that makes $P$ self-adjoint is $\mu=e^{-\alpha_0}\mathrm{vol}_{g_0}$. On the other hand $\Phi(1,\cdot,\cdot)$ is that inner product, since $1=\ker P$ and $\Phi(1,u,v)=\langle u,v\rangle_{L^2(\mu)}$. Thus $\mu=e^{-\alpha_0}\mathrm{vol}_g$, so $\alpha=\alpha_0+c$. A Witten operator on a closed manifold is determined by $(g,\alpha)$ up to that constant. The $C^k$ estimate follows from Lemma 14 and the elliptic regularity of $\log(d\mu/d\mathrm{vol}_g)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark} Lemma~\ref{lma-4} is recognition, not construction: it uses that $P$ was Witten. The two measures $\mu$ and $\mathrm{vol}_g$ are the only source of $\alpha$. The packet does not store $\alpha$ separately.
\end{remark}

\begin{corollary}
 Combining Lemmas 9, 14, and 15, the packet determines a Riemannian manifold $(\Sigma,g_\Sigma)$ in $\mathcal{V}^\ast$ and a weight $\alpha_\Sigma$ on $\Sigma$. Evaluation pulls these back to $(g,\alpha)$ exactly when $R=0$, and to within $C(d,k)\lVert R\rVert_{C^k(\Sigma)}$ when $\Delta<\Delta_*$.
\end{corollary}


\section{Recognition}
The lemmas of §§3–5 assemble into Theorem~\ref{thm-1} and Theorem~\ref{thm-2}.

\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem~\ref{thm-1}]
Assume $R=0$. Lemma 5 gives $\Gamma$ on $\mathcal{V}$ as a function of $(P,\Phi)$, with remainder zero. Lemma 9 identifies $X$ with the character space $\Sigma\subset\mathcal{V}^\ast$ of the finite algebra $(\mathcal{V},m)$ by a $C^\infty$ diffeomorphism $\iota$. Lemma 14 dualizes $\Gamma$ to a Riemannian metric $g_\Sigma$ on $\Sigma$ with $\iota^*g_\Sigma=g$. Lemma 15 reads $\alpha$ from $\mu/\mathrm{vol}_g$, up to an additive constant, and identifies $P$ with the Witten operator of that pair. Thus $\iota$ is a $C^\infty$ Riemannian isometry, and the packet determines $(X,g,\alpha)$ up to that isometry and the constant on $\alpha$. Theorem 21 upgrades the unweighted case to an orbit decomposition, and to a sphere when $d=n+1$.
Residual freedom is an orthogonal change of basis of $\mathcal{V}$ preserving $\Phi$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem~\ref{thm-2}]
Lemma 5 gives $\Gamma$ on $\mathcal{V}$ with remainder $R$. Under the hypothesis of (i), Lemma 9(i) produces a $C^1$ diffeomorphism $\iota:X\to\Sigma\subset\mathcal{V}^*$ and identifies $\lVert R\rVert_{C^k(\Sigma)}$ with $\lVert R\rVert_{C^k(X)}$ up to $C(d,k)$. Lemma 14 upgrades the cometric error to
$$\lVert\iota^*g_\Sigma-g\rVert_{C^k}\le C(d,k)\,\lVert R\rVert_{C^k(\Sigma)}.$$
Lemma 15 gives the same bound for $\alpha_\Sigma\circ\iota-\alpha-c$. Under the hypothesis of (ii), Lemma 9(ii) converts $\lVert t\rVert_{C^1}$ and $\lVert R\rVert_{C^k}$ into multiples of $\Delta$, and (i) applies. If $R=0$, both parts reduce to Theorem 1.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Two packets for the same operator at different admissible cutoffs produce isometric copies if both have $R=0$, and almost-isometric copies on the scale of the two values of $\lVert R\rVert_{C^k(\Sigma)}$ otherwise. The theorems do not identify merely isospectral Witten operators.
\end{remark}

\section{The Unweighted First Window on the Sphere}
This section is Theorems 1 and 2 on a single window: $\alpha=0$, $\Lambda=n$, $\dim\mathcal{V}=n+1$, and products of the window supported in slots $\{0,2n+2\}$ when $R=0$.
Let $\{x_a\}_{a=0}^n$ be an $L^2$-orthonormal frame in $\mathcal{V}$ as in Theorem 3, and write $F=(x_0,\dots,x_n):X\to\mathbb{R}^{n+1}$.

\begin{lemma} Assume the hypotheses of Theorem 3. Then $\sum_a x_a^2=1$, $\Gamma(x_a,x_b)=\delta_{ab}-x_ax_b$, and $dF$ has rank $n$ at every point. Consequently $F(X)$ is a closed embedded hypersurface in the unit sphere of $\mathbb{R}^{n+1}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The quadric $\sum_a x_a^2=1$ is given. Differentiating and using $Px_a=nx_a$ with (A1) yields
$$2\Gamma(x_a,x_b)=n x_ax_b+n x_bx_a-P(x_ax_b).$$
By hypothesis $x_ax_b-\delta_{ab}/(n+1)$ is an eigenfunction at $2n+2$, and $P(1)=0$, so
$$P(x_ax_b)=(2n+2)\Bigl(x_ax_b-\frac{\delta_{ab}}{n+1}\Bigr).$$
Hence $\Gamma(x_a,x_b)=\delta_{ab}-x_ax_b$. The matrix $\delta_{ab}-x_ax_b$ has rank $n$ on the unit sphere, and $\{dx_a\}$ spans $T^*X$ by hypothesis, which is W2 for this frame. W1 follows: $F$ is an immersion, $X$ is closed, and $F(X)$ lies on $S^n$, so $F$ is a covering onto its image. Simple connectedness of $S^n$ for $n\ge 2$, or a direct check for $n=1$, makes $F$ a diffeomorphism onto $S^n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem~\ref{thm-a}] Lemma 19 and Theorem 1 give that $X$ is isometric to the unit round sphere. This is the case $d=n+1$, $Q\equiv 1$ of Lemma 26. The metric dual to $\Gamma(x_a,x_b)=\delta_{ab}-x_ax_b$ is the round metric of radius $1$, and $\alpha=0$ because $\mu=\mathrm{vol}_g$. Thus $P=-\Delta$. The frame spans the first harmonics because it is an $(n+1)$-dimensional eigenspace at eigenvalue $n$. If the first eigenvalue is $\lambda$ rather than $n$, the same identities with the pair $(\lambda,2\lambda+2\lambda/n)$ rescale the radius to $\sqrt{n/\lambda}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem~\ref{thm-b}]
The isolation hypothesis \(w<\lambda_{n+2}-\lambda_{n+1}\) makes \(\mathcal{V}\) a
spectral window of dimension \(n+1\). Standing W1 and W2 apply to this
block. Write \(\lambda_a=n+\varepsilon_a\), so \(\lvert\varepsilon_a\rvert\le\delta_w\).

Lemma 5 on the cluster frame, with model projector
\(\Pi_{\mathrm{sph}}^\eta\), gives
\[
2\Gamma(x_a,x_b)=(\lambda_a+\lambda_b)x_ax_b-P\bigl(m(x_a,x_b)\bigr)+R_{\Lambda'}
\]
and
\[
\bigl\lVert\Gamma(x_a,x_b)-(\delta_{ab}-x_ax_b)\bigr\rVert_{L^2}
\le C(n,\eta)\bigl(\delta_w+\delta_\Phi+\Delta_{\mathrm{fr}}\bigr).
\]
Here \(\delta_\Phi\) is mass of \(\Pi_{\Lambda'}(x_ax_b)\) off
\(\Pi_{\mathrm{sph}}^\eta\), and \(\Delta_{\mathrm{fr}}\) is the tail above
\(\Lambda'\). The visible slot has dimension \(O(n^2)\) for fixed
\(\eta,\Lambda'\). Remarks 6–7 upgrade the estimate to \(C^0\):
\[
\bigl\lVert\Gamma(x_a,x_b)-(\delta_{ab}-x_ax_b)\bigr\rVert_{C^0}
\le C(n,\eta,\Lambda')\bigl(\delta_w+\delta_\Phi+\Delta_{\mathrm{fr}}\bigr).
\]
If \(\Lambda'=2n+2+\eta\), the constant depends only on \(n\) and \(\eta\).

The deficit \(\delta_1\) puts \(\sum_a x_a^2=1+O_{C^0}(\delta_1)\), so
evaluation \(F=(x_1,\dots,x_{n+1})\) lands in a \(C^0\)-neighborhood of
the unit sphere in \(\mathcal{V}^*\). Gram–Schmidt absorbs
\(\delta_{\mathrm{on}}\) into \(\delta_1\) and \(\delta_\Phi\) at cost
\(C(n)\). The rank gap \(\delta_{\mathrm{rank}}\) keeps \(dF\) of rank \(n\)
for \(\delta<\delta_*(n,\eta)\).

Lemma 9, with \(d=n+1\) and a finite slot, produces a \(C^1\)
diffeomorphism of \(X\) onto a graph over \(S^n\). Theorem 2(ii) yields
\[
d_{\mathrm{GH}}(X,S^n)+d_{C^0}(g,g_{\mathrm{rd}})
\le C(n,\eta,\Lambda')\,\delta.
\]
No Ricci bound is used. Diameter control is that graph, not Cheng
comparison.

Lemma 5 already records the Kato reduction: on a small \(C^2\) ball
about \(g_{\mathrm{rd}}\), written as a divergence-form operator on
\(L^2(d\mathrm{vol}_{g_{\mathrm{rd}}})\), the contours about \(0\),
\(n\), and \(2n+2\) miss \(\operatorname{Spec}(\Delta_g)\)
[Kat95, Ch.~IV]. Thus the three gaps persist,
\(\delta\le C(n,\eta)\,\|g-g_{\mathrm{rd}}\|_{C^2}\), and Theorem 4
applies on that ball in \(\mathcal{M}^2(M)\) for \(M\simeq S^n\).
Individual eigenvalues in the cluster may split; only the joint span
\(\mathcal{V}\) is used. The specialisation \(w=0\), \(\eta\to 0\),
\(\Lambda'=2n+2+\eta\) recovers exact projectors
\(\Pi_0+\Pi_{2n+2}\) and the single-eigenspace stability of Theorem 3.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Obata characterizes $S^n$ by $\operatorname{Ric}\ge(n-1)g$ and $\lambda_1=n$, using one eigenfunction \cite{Oba62}. Theorems 3 and 4 use an $(n+1)$-frame and its products. Quantitative Obata is Ricci-side stability of Obata \cite{CMS}. Theorem 4 is frame-side stability of Theorem 3. They are not the same theorem.
\end{remark}

\section{What the Packet Does Not Determine}
The packet of §2 is a finite piece of the multiplication table of $P$. It does not determine the rest of $\sigma(P)$. Two Witten operators with the same window products and different high eigenvalues are not distinguished.

It does not determine a potential. The standing category is Witten: $V=0$. A Schrödinger term is a different operator and a different paper \cite{Sch26}.

It does not yield spectral uniqueness. Two operators with the same spectrum, or the same $\sigma(P)\cap[0,\Lambda']$, need not be isometric; the missing data are $\Phi$ and $R$. Two operators with the same exact packet ($R=0$) are isometric. Two operators with the same approximate packet need not be; they are $C^k$-close on the scale of $\lVert R\rVert_{C^k(\Sigma)}$.

It does not yield a constant depending only on $n$ in Theorem 2. The factor $C(d,k)$ depends on the window dimension. A universal $C(n)$ when $\Lambda'=2n+2$ appears only in Theorem 4, where $d=n+1$ is fixed.

It does not use Ricci curvature, a Weyl law, or the full table $\Phi$ on $\bigoplus_n\ker(P-\lambda_n)$. Those are the inputs of Obata theorems \cite{Oba62,CMS}, of eigenvalue asymptotics, and of the global comparison theorem \cite{Sch24,Sch26}, respectively. The present arguments use a window frame and its products.

Two packets for the same operator at different admissible cutoffs are consistent with Theorems 1 and 2. That consistency is not part of one packet; each packet has its own $(\Lambda,\Lambda',R)$.

An exact unweighted window is not shown to be a single homogeneous space except when $d=n+1$ and $Q$ is constant.

Sunada pairs remain non-isometric and therefore cannot share an exact packet; §9 does not contradict §8.

The contribution of the paper is the converse direction inside this class: a finite, explicitly tailed window already reconstructs a Witten pair, and the unit round sphere is the minimal unweighted instance of that reconstruction.

\section{Orbits of an Exact Unweighted Window}
Assume throughout this section that $\alpha=0$, that $R=0$, and that W1 and W2 hold. Thus $P=-\Delta_g$, products of the window land in $\mathcal{V}'$, and evaluation $\iota:X\to\mathcal{V}^*$ is a smooth embedding whose differentials span $T^*X$. Let $d=\dim\mathcal{V}$ and fix an $L^2$-orthonormal frame $\{u_a\}_{a=1}^d$ of $\mathcal{V}$. Write $K=\mathrm{Isom}(X,g)$ and $K^\circ$ for its identity component.

\begin{theorem}
\label{thm-exact}
$K$ is a compact Lie group of isometries of $(X,g)$. The identity component acts real-analytically, and $X$ is a union of $G^\circ$-orbits (Lemma 25). If $\dim\mathcal{V}=n+1$ and $Q$ is a positive constant, then $G^\circ$ is transitive and $X$ is a sphere (Lemma 26). In general the principal set is a fibre bundle with fibre a compact homogeneous space. Residual packet freedom remains the compact group of orthogonal changes of frame of $\mathcal{V}$ that preserve $\Phi$.
\end{theorem}

Theorem 21 does not assume a tower and does not use Theorem 32.

\begin{lemma}[Myers–Steenrod and the window] $K$ is a compact Lie group \cite{MS39}. Pullback by $K$ preserves every eigenspace of $P$, hence preserves $\mathcal{V}$ and $\mathcal{V}'$, and $\iota$ is $K$-equivariant. The reconstructed cometric $\Gamma$ of Lemma 5 and the metric of Lemma 14 are $K$-invariant.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}Myers–Steenrod: the isometry group of a compact Riemannian manifold is a compact Lie group acting smoothly. An isometry commutes with $\Delta_g$, so it preserves eigenspaces and the $L^2$ pairing. It therefore acts on $\mathcal{V}$ and on $\mathcal{V}'$, and
$$\iota(k\cdot x)(u)=u(k^{-1}x)=(k^*\iota(x))(u),$$
so $\iota$ is equivariant. Lemma 5 with $R=0$ gives $\Gamma(u,v)$ as a bilinear combination of products and of $P$ on $\mathcal{V}$. Both are $K$-invariant, so $\Gamma$ is. Lemma 14 dualizes $\Gamma$ to $g$, which is therefore $K$-invariant.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[the quadratic $Q$] Let
$$Q:=\sum_{a=1}^d u_a^2\in C^\infty(X).$$
Then $Q\in\mathcal{V}'$. If $\mathcal{V}$ is contained in a single eigenspace of eigenvalue $\lambda$, then
$$PQ=2\lambda Q-2\sum_{a=1}^d\Gamma(u_a,u_a).$$
If in addition $\sum_a\Gamma(u_a,u_a)=\lambda Q$, then $Q$ is constant and $\iota(X)$ lies on a sphere in $\mathcal{V}^*$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Each product $u_au_a$ lies in $\mathcal{V}'$ because $R=0$, so $Q\in\mathcal{V}'$. The identity (A1) gives
$$P(u_a^2)=2u_aPu_a-2\Gamma(u_a,u_a).$$
Summing and using $Pu_a=\lambda u_a$ yields the displayed formula. If the trace identity $\sum_a\Gamma(u_a,u_a)=\lambda Q$ holds, then $PQ=0$. Connected $X$ and $\ker P=\mathrm{span}\{1\}$ force $Q$ constant. The value of $\iota$ then lies on the sphere of radius $\sqrt{Q}$ in $\mathcal{V}^*$.
\end{proof}

On the first spherical window the trace identity is $\sum_a\Gamma(x_a,x_a)=n=n\sum_a x_a^2$, so Lemma 22 recovers the quadric of Lemma 19. The trace identity $\sum_a\Gamma(u_a,u_a)=\lambda Q$ is the Takahashi condition that $\iota$ be a minimal immersion into a sphere when $\mathcal{V}$ is one eigenspace \cite{Tak66}. If several eigenvalues appear and $\iota$ is isometric, $\iota$ is a Garay map \cite{Gar90}. The image lies in a product of spheres when each slot has constant \(Q_j\) and the joint frame is Parseval (Theorem 32). Neither identity is assumed in Theorem 21.

\begin{lemma}[the linear automorphism group]
Let $m:\mathcal{V}\times\mathcal{V}\to\mathcal{V}'$ be the multiplication $m(u,v)=uv$ of the exact packet, and let
$$Z:=\bigl\{\xi\in\mathcal{V}^*:\ \xi(u)\xi(v)=\eta_\xi(m(u,v))\ \text{for all }u,v\in\mathcal{V}\bigr\},$$
where $\eta_\xi\in(\mathcal{V}')^*$ is the unique linear extension of $\xi$ along $m$ furnished by $R=0$ (evaluation of $\mathcal{V}'$ on $X$, transported by Lemma 9). Let
$$G:=\bigl\{A\in\mathrm{O}(\mathcal{V}^*):\ A\cdot Z=Z\bigr\}.$$
Then $G$ is a compact Lie group, $\iota(X)$ is a union of connected components of the smooth locus of $Z$, and $G$ acts isometrically on $(X,g)$. The image of $K$ in $\mathrm{O}(\mathcal{V}^*)$ lands in $G$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The conditions cutting out $Z$ are quadratic in $\xi$, so $Z$ is a real-algebraic set. $G$ is a closed subgroup of $\mathrm{O}(d)$, hence a compact Lie group. Lemma 9 with $t=0$ identifies $\iota(X)$ with the smooth characters of $(\mathcal{V},m)$, which is precisely the smooth locus of $Z$ along the component containing $\iota(X)$. W1 says that component is a smooth embedded copy of $X$.
If $A\in G$, then $A$ preserves the multiplication tensor $m$ up to the orthogonal action on $\mathcal{V}'$ that makes the identities $\xi(u)\xi(v)=\eta(m(u,v))$ invariant. Consequently $A$ preserves $\Gamma$, which by Lemma 5 is a bilinear combination of $m$ and of $P|_{\mathcal{V}}$. The spectrum of $P$ on $\mathcal{V}$ is part of the packet and is $G$-invariant. Lemma 14 therefore gives that $A$ acts by an isometry of $g$. Lemma 21 puts the image of $K$ inside $G$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[orbits]
$G^\circ$ acts real-analytically on $X$. All orbits in a connected component of the principal set have the same dimension. Consequently $X$ is a disjoint union of $G^\circ$-orbits, and $G^\circ$ is transitive on $X$ if and only if some orbit is open in $X$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The metric of Lemma 14 is dual to $\Gamma$, and $\Gamma$ is a bilinear combination of the packet product $m$ and of $P|_{\mathcal{V}}$ (Lemma 5 with $R=0$). Both are real-analytic, so $g$ is real-analytic and $X$ is a compact real-analytic Riemannian manifold. $G$ is a compact linear algebraic subgroup of $\mathrm{O}(\mathcal{V}^*)$, hence a compact Lie group, and its action on $Z^{\mathrm{sm}}$ is algebraic, therefore real-analytic. Restricting to the component $\iota(X)$ gives a real-analytic action on $X$.

Orbits of a compact Lie group are compact embedded submanifolds. The principal orbit theorem supplies an open dense principal set $X^{\mathrm{prin}}$ on which all orbits have the same dimension $r\le n$ and the same type. Unique continuation for real-analytic maps: an orbit of dimension $r$ that meets $X^{\mathrm{prin}}$ cannot drop dimension on a nonempty open subset of $X$. Thus every orbit in the given connected component of $X^{\mathrm{prin}}$ has dimension $r$, and $X$ is a union of such orbits together with lower-dimensional singular orbits of measure zero.

If some orbit $\mathcal{O}$ is open in $X$, then $\mathcal{O}$ is also closed (compact), so connectedness of $X$ gives $\mathcal{O}=X$. Conversely, a transitive action has a single orbit, which is open.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[open orbit when $d=n+1$]
Assume in addition that \(\dim\mathcal{V}=n+1\) and that the function
\(Q=\sum_a u_a^2\) is a positive constant. Then \(\iota(X)\) is the sphere
of radius \(\sqrt{Q}\) in \(\mathcal{V}^*\), \(G^\circ\) contains a
transitive action on that sphere, and \(G^\circ\) is transitive on \(X\).
In particular \(X\) is diffeomorphic to a sphere, and the metric is
round if in addition \(\mathcal{V}\) is one eigenspace and the trace
identity of Lemma 23 holds.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The identity \(\lvert\iota\rvert^2=Q\) puts \(\iota(X)\) on the sphere
\(S\) of radius \(\sqrt{Q}\) in the \((n+1)\)-dimensional space
\(\mathcal{V}^*\). W1 says \(\iota\) is an embedding of an \(n\)-manifold,
so \(\iota(X)\) is open in \(S\). \(S\) is connected, so \(\iota(X)=S\).
The orthogonal group \(\mathrm{O}(n+1)\) preserves \(S\) and acts
transitively on it. Restricting to the identity component and applying
Lemma 25 gives transitivity on \(X\). Thus \(X\) is diffeomorphic to a
sphere.

If moreover \(\mathcal{V}\) is a single eigenspace and
\(\sum_a\Gamma(u_a,u_a)=\lambda Q\), Lemma 23 puts \(\iota\) on a sphere
in the Takahashi sense and the reconstructed metric of Lemma 14 is
round of radius \(\sqrt{Q}\). In the first spherical window this is
Lemma 19: \(\Gamma(x_a,x_b)=\delta_{ab}-x_ax_b\) and radius \(1\).
Those extra identities are the data of Theorem 3; they are not
assumed in Theorem 21 beyond \(Q\) constant.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[cubic slot]
Assume \(\alpha=0\), \(R=0\), W1, W2, and that \(\mathcal{V}\) is a single
eigenspace of eigenvalue \(\lambda\) and dimension \(d\); write
\(Q=\sum_a u_a^2\). If in addition the cubic slot is finite,
\[
\mathcal{V}\cdot\mathcal{V}'\subset\mathcal{V}'',
\]
with \(\mathcal{V}''\) a finite spectral window, then \(II\), computed in
\(\mathcal{V}^*\) from the frame \(\{u_a\}\), is a packet tensor with
coefficients in \(\mathcal{V}'\), and \(\nabla II\) is a packet tensor
with coefficients in \(\mathcal{V}''\).
If moreover the trace identity of Lemma 23 holds,
\(\sum_a\Gamma(u_a,u_a)=\lambda Q\), then Takahashi applies: \(Q\equiv n/\lambda\)
and \(\iota:X\to S^{d-1}(\sqrt{n/\lambda})\) is a full minimal immersion, and
the second fundamental form in that sphere is the packet tensor \(II_S\).
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Lemma 5 with \(R=0\) puts \(\Gamma(u_a,u_b)\) in \(\mathcal{V}'\). The
second fundamental form of \(\iota(X)\subset\mathcal{V}^*\) is assembled
from Hessians of the coordinate functions, hence from \(\Gamma\) by the
Gauss formula, so \(II\) has coefficients in \(\mathcal{V}'\).
Differentiating the identity
\[
Z\,\Gamma(u_a,u_b)=\mathrm{Hess}\,u_a(Z,\nabla u_b)+\mathrm{Hess}\,u_b(Z,\nabla u_a)
\]
produces coefficients \(\Gamma(u,f)\) for \(u\in\mathcal{V}\) and
\(f\in\mathcal{V}'\). Leibniz writes those as
\[
2\Gamma(u,f)=\lambda uf+u\,Pf-P(uf).
\]
The product \(uf\) lies in \(\mathcal{V}''\) by hypothesis, and \(P\)
preserves \(\mathcal{V}''\). Thus every coefficient of \(\nabla II\)
lies in \(\mathcal{V}''\).

The trace identity \(\sum_a\Gamma(u_a,u_a)=\lambda Q\) is the condition
that \(\iota\) be isometric into \(\mathcal{V}^*\). Combined with
\(\Delta u_a=-\lambda u_a\), Takahashi supplies a sphere of radius
\(\sqrt{n/\lambda}\) and minimality there \cite{Tak66}. The spherical second
fundamental form \(II_S\) is then the packet tensor already obtained.
Neither the trace identity nor minimality is assumed in Theorem 21.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[tower]
Assume the hypotheses of Lemma 27, and let
  \(\mathcal{V}_0=\mathcal{V},\qquad
    \mathcal{V}_k=\bigoplus_{\lambda\le\Lambda_k}\ker(P-\lambda),
  \)
be a tower of windows satisfying \(\mathcal{V}_j\cdot\mathcal{V}_k\subset\mathcal{V}_{j+k}\).
Then every covariant derivative \(\nabla^m II_S\) has coefficients in \(\mathcal{V}_{m+2}\).

The tower cannot stabilize. If \(\bigcup_k\mathcal{V}_k=A\) were finite-dimensional, then
\(A\) would be a unital subalgebra of \(C(X)\). Any finite-dimensional commutative
subalgebra of \(C(X)\) is semisimple and therefore has finite Gelfand spectrum.
W1 implies that \(A\) separates points, so \(X\) would be finite, contradicting
connectedness and \(\dim X\ge 1\).

If, in addition, the multiplication of the tower is the Clebsch–Gordan
decomposition of a compact Lie group \(G\) acting smoothly on \(X\), then
\(\iota\) is a \(G\)-equivariant eigenmap and \(X\) is a union of \(G\)-orbits.
If in addition the hypotheses of Theorem 32 hold, the orbit is an
\(s\)-representation orbit \cite{Fer80, Tak81, OlmSan93}; \cite{TK68} is the
standard embedding of an \(R\)-space. Parallelism is not claimed
for a general compact-group orbit.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The jet statement is the product rule and Codazzi applied to the
packet expression for \(II_S\) in Lemma 27, once at each rung of the tower.
The stabilization clause is the Gelfand spectrum of a finite-dimensional
subalgebra of \(C(X)\), as above. The graded case is Peter–Weyl: the
windows are isotypic components, \(\iota\) intertwines the \(G\)-actions,
and \cite{TK68} applies exactly to \(R\)-space orbits.


\end{proof}

If $\mathcal{V}=\bigoplus_i\mathcal{V}_{\lambda_i}$ with more than one eigenvalue, Takahashi applies factorwise: each $\iota_{\lambda_i}(X)$ lies in a sphere in $\mathcal{V}_{\lambda_i}^*$, and $\iota(X)$ lies in a product of spheres. The single-quadric argument of Lemma 26 does not run. Constancy of the total $Q=\sum_i Q_i$ is a sum of several sphere equations and does not make $G=\mathrm{O}(\dim\mathcal{V})$.

If $\iota$ is isometric and $\mathcal{V}$ has several eigenvalues, then $\iota$ is a Garay map. The image lies in a product of spheres only after each spectral projector is constant on the diagonal, which is not implied by $R=0$. In codimension one the complete list is spheres and cylinders \cite{Gar90}.

A tower of height \(2\) suffices for the third jet of a window
supported in \(\mathcal{V}_0\): \(\mathcal{V}_0\cdot\mathcal{V}_0\subset\mathcal{V}_1\)
puts \(\Gamma(F,F)\) in \(\mathcal{V}_1\), and \(Z\cdot\Gamma(F,F)\) is a
derivative of that slot, not a product \(\mathcal{V}_1\cdot\mathcal{V}_1\).
If \(\iota\) also uses \(\mathcal{V}_1\), close the mixed products
\(\mathcal{V}_0\cdot\mathcal{V}_1\subset\mathcal{V}_1\) (or \(\mathcal{V}_2\)).
Stabilisation at \(\mathcal{V}_1\) is the two-step exact algebra.
One-step \(R=0\) without \(\mathcal{V}_1\) in the embedding is
exactly Theorem~3 when \(\dim\mathcal{V}_0=n+1\).

\begin{remark} Lemmas 27–28 are not used in Theorems 1–4. One exact pair does not give the tower; the torus tower is the abelian case of the last clause.
The cubic slot and the tower hold for every CROSS first window and every Peter–Weyl window. They are the extra structure that upgrades packet $II_S$ to a compact group orbit. Combined with a Chern–do Carmo–Kobayashi bound on $\lvert II_S\rvert^2$ \cite{CDK70}, Lemma 27 alone already forces $\nabla II_S=0$ in the pinched range. That pinching is not used below. Parallelism of \(\mathrm{II}\) for a tower is Theorem 32. Theorem 21 does not use either lemma.
\end{remark}

\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem 21] Lemmas 22–24 give a compact Lie group of isometries of $(X,g)$ and a compact linear group $G$ acting on $\iota(X)$. Lemma 25 gives that $X$ is a union of $G^\circ$-orbits, and that transitivity is equivalent to the existence of an open orbit. Lemma 26 supplies that open orbit when $\dim\mathcal{V}=n+1$ and $Q$ is constant, which is the first window of Theorems 3 and 21 in the spherical case. In general, set $H=\mathrm{Stab}_{K^\circ}(x)$ along a principal orbit; the principal set is a fibre bundle with fibre $K^\circ/H$. Finite covers and ineffective kernels are as before.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}Theorem 21 in full generality is the orbit decomposition of Lemma 25. Transitivity is proved when $d=n+1$ and $Q$ is constant, which is the case already used by Lemma 19 and Theorem 3. Theorem 32 gives a stronger conclusion (symmetric orbit, not merely a
union of orbits) under the exact-tower and Parseval hypotheses, which Theorem 21 does not assume. A general exact window is a union of compact orbits of a compact linear group of isometries; it is a single homogeneous space once an open orbit is known to exist. Compact hyperbolic surfaces remain excluded: they have no exact finite window satisfying W1.
\end{remark}

\subsection{Parseval Towers and Parallel \(\mathrm{II}\)}
Assume \(\alpha=0\) and an exact, finite tower of unweighted windows
\[
\mathcal{V}=\mathcal{V}_0\subset\cdots\subset\mathcal{V}_N,
\]
meaning \(\mathcal{V}_j\cdot\mathcal{V}_k\subset\mathcal{V}_{j+k}\) for
\(j+k\le N\) and \(\mathcal{V}_N\cdot\mathcal{V}_1\subset\mathcal{V}_N\).
Write \(\mathcal{E}_j=\mathcal{V}_j\ominus\mathcal{V}_{j-1}\). Assume each
slot admits an \(L^2\)-orthonormal basis \(\{u^{(j)}_a\}\) with constant
quadratic \(Q_j=\sum_a(u^{(j)}_a)^2\), and that the joint frame is
Parseval:
\[
\sum_{j,a} du^{(j)}_a\otimes du^{(j)}_a=g.
\]
Let \(F_j=(u^{(j)}_a)_a\) and \(F=(F_1,\dots,F_N)\). One-step \(R=0\)
is the case \(N=1\) without the higher inclusions; it does not imply
an exact tower.

\begin{theorem}
Under these hypotheses, \(\iota=F\) is a homothetic embedding of
\((X,g)\) into a product of spheres, \(\nabla\mathrm{II}=0\), and
\(\iota(X)\) is an orbit of an isotropy representation of a compact
symmetric space, or a product of such orbits.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Each coordinate of \(F_j\) is an eigenfunction of \(-\Delta\) and
\(Q_j\) is constant, so Takahashi's theorem places \(F_j(X)\) on the
sphere of radius \(\sqrt{Q_j}\) in the Euclidean space of
\(\mathcal{E}_j\) \cite{Tak66}. Parseval makes \(\iota=F\) a homothety of
\((X,g)\) into the product of those spheres \cite{Gar90}. Thus \(\mathrm{II}\)
is the second fundamental form of a Riemannian immersion
\(X\to\mathbb{R}^d\).

Leibniz and exactness of the tower give, for \(u\in\mathcal{E}_j\) and
\(v\in\mathcal{E}_k\) with \(j+k\le N\),
\[
uv\in\mathcal{V}_{j+k}\subset\operatorname{span}\{F_\ell\}.
\]
The Hessian of a coordinate of \(F\) is computed from
\(2\Gamma(u,v)=uPv+vPu-P(uv)\). The right-hand side lies in the
tower, so the \(2\)-jet of \(F\) takes values in
\(\operatorname{span}\{F_\ell\}\). Differentiating the eigen-equation
\(\Delta F_j=\lambda_j F_j\) and substituting the \(2\)-jet puts every
third derivative of \(F\) in the same span. Hence the normal space of
\(\iota(X)\) in \(\mathbb{R}^d\) is spanned by a finite list of
eigenfunctions, \(K\)-invariant by Theorem 21, and closed under
directional derivatives. That list is a parallel normal subbundle, so
\(\nabla\mathrm{II}=0\). For more details on these computations see Section 9.2 below.

A compact full immersion with \(\nabla\mathrm{II}=0\) into Euclidean
space is an orbit of an \(s\)-representation (isotropy representation
of a compact symmetric space), or a product of such orbits
\cite{Fer80, Tak81, OlmSan93}. The product-of-spheres ambient embeds
standardly in Euclidean space, so the classification applies.

The Parseval identity is automatic when \(X\) is compact irreducible
symmetric and each \(\mathcal{E}_j\) is a full isotypic slot: \(Q_j\)
is invariant, hence constant, and \(\iota^*g_{\mathrm{euc}}\) is
invariant, hence a multiple of \(g\). It may fail for a left-invariant
metric that is not bi-invariant, for an incomplete slot, or for an
unbalanced window on a Riemannian product. In those cases Theorem 21
still supplies a compact group of isometries of \(g_\Gamma\).
\end{proof}

Theorem 21 does not assume a tower and does not use Theorem 32.
Whether every one-step exact unweighted window extends to an exact
tower is left open.

\subsection{Jets of an exact tower}
Let \(\mathcal{V}=\mathcal{V}_0\subset\cdots\subset\mathcal{V}_N\) be an
exact unweighted tower, \(\alpha=0\), \(P=-\Delta\), and let
\(F=(F_1,\dots,F_N)\) be the joint eigenframe of the slots
\(\mathcal{E}_j=\mathcal{V}_j\ominus\mathcal{V}_{j-1}\), so
\(\Delta F_j=\lambda_j F_j\). Write \(\mathcal{A}=\operatorname{span}\{F_\ell\}
=\mathcal{V}_N\). Exactness means
\[
\mathcal{V}_j\cdot\mathcal{V}_k\subset\mathcal{V}_{j+k}\qquad(j+k\le N),
\qquad
\mathcal{V}_N\cdot\mathcal{V}_1\subset\mathcal{V}_N,
\]
hence \(\mathcal{A}\) is an algebra and \(P(\mathcal{A})\subset\mathcal{A}\).

\paragraph{First derivatives.}
For \(u\in\mathcal{A}\) one has \(du(X)=X(u)\). In particular every
first derivative of a coordinate of \(F\) is a directional derivative of
an element of \(\mathcal{A}\).

\paragraph{\(2\)-jet.}
Leibniz for \(P=-\Delta\) on functions is
\[
2\Gamma(u,v)=uPv+vPu-P(uv)=g(\nabla u,\nabla v).
\]
If \(u\in\mathcal{E}_j\) and \(v\in\mathcal{E}_k\) with \(j+k\le N\),
then \(uv\in\mathcal{V}_{j+k}\subset\mathcal{A}\) and \(Pu,Pv\in\mathcal{A}\),
so \(\Gamma(u,v)\in\mathcal{A}\). In coordinates,
\[
\operatorname{Hess}u(X,Y)=X(Yu)-\nabla_X Y(u).
\]
The Bochner identity on an eigenfunction \(Pu=\lambda u\) reads
\[
\operatorname{Hess}u+\tfrac{\lambda}{n}u\,g=\text{trace-free part},
\]
and the trace-free part is recovered from \(\Gamma\) by polarisation.
Thus every second partial of a coordinate of \(F\) is a linear
combination of elements of \(\mathcal{A}\): the \(2\)-jet of \(F\)
takes values in \(\mathcal{A}\).

\paragraph{\(3\)-jet.}
The Euclidean third jet of \(F\) along vector fields \(X,Y,Z\) is
\[
D_ZD_X\,dF(Y)
=dF\bigl(\nabla_Z\nabla_XY\bigr)
+\mathrm{II}\bigl(Z,\nabla_XY\bigr)
+D_Z\bigl(\mathrm{II}(X,Y)\bigr).
\]
Split the last term by Weingarten:
\[
D_Z\bigl(\mathrm{II}(X,Y)\bigr)
=\nabla^\perp_Z\bigl(\mathrm{II}(X,Y)\bigr)
-A_{\mathrm{II}(X,Y)}Z,
\]
so
\begin{align*}
D_ZD_X\,dF(Y)
&=dF\bigl(\nabla_Z\nabla_XY-A_{\mathrm{II}(X,Y)}Z\bigr)\\
&\qquad+\mathrm{II}\bigl(Z,\nabla_XY\bigr)
+(\nabla_Z\mathrm{II})(X,Y)
+\mathrm{II}(\nabla_ZX,Y)
+\mathrm{II}(X,\nabla_ZY).
\end{align*}
The first line is tangent to \(\iota(X)\). On a Parseval exact tower
each spherical factor contributes
\[
\mathrm{II}_j(X,Y)
=-\frac1{Q_j}\,\Gamma(F_j,F_j)(X,Y)\,F_j,
\]
hence
\begin{align*}
(\nabla_Z\mathrm{II}_j)(X,Y)
&=-\frac1{Q_j}\bigl(Z\cdot\Gamma(F_j,F_j)(X,Y)\bigr)F_j\\
&\qquad-\frac1{Q_j}\Gamma(F_j,F_j)(X,Y)\,dF_j(Z)^\top,
\end{align*}
where the second summand is tangent to the \(j\)-th sphere
(Weingarten on that factor). The coefficient derivative is
\[
Z\cdot\Gamma(F_j,F_j)(X,Y)
=\operatorname{Hess}\Gamma(F_j,F_j)(Z,\,\cdot\,)(X,Y)
+\Gamma(F_j,F_j)(\nabla_ZX,Y)
+\Gamma(F_j,F_j)(X,\nabla_ZY).
\]
Leibniz puts \(\Gamma(F_j,F_j)(\xi,\eta)\in\mathcal{A}\) for
\(\xi,\eta\in\mathfrak{X}(X)\), so the Hessian of that coefficient
is the Hessian of a function in \(\mathcal{A}\) and takes values
in \(\mathcal{A}\) after tracing against \(g\). Collecting normal
parts,
\[
\bigl(D_ZD_X\,dF(Y)\bigr)^\perp
=\sum_j\Biggl(
-\frac1{Q_j}\bigl(Z\cdot\Gamma(F_j,F_j)(X,Y)\bigr)
-\frac1{Q_j}\Gamma(F_j,F_j)\bigl(Z,\nabla_XY\bigr)
\Biggr)F_j,
\]
which lies in \(\operatorname{span}\{F_\ell\}\). That is the
explicit third-jet formula used for \(\nabla\mathrm{II}=0\).

\paragraph{Normal bundle.}
View \(\iota=F:X\to\mathbb{R}^d\). The Euclidean second derivative of
\(F\) splits as \(\nabla dF=\mathrm{II}\oplus\nabla^T dF\). The
tangential part is the Levi-Civita connection of \(g\) (Parseval). The
normal part \(\mathrm{II}(X,Y)\) is the component of
\(\operatorname{Hess}F(X,Y)\) orthogonal to \(T\iota(X)\). By the
\(2\)-jet step that vector is a linear combination of the coordinates
of \(F\), so
\[
N_{\iota(x)}\iota(X)\subset\mathcal{A}_x\subset\mathbb{R}^d.
\]
The \(3\)-jet step says that a directional derivative of such a normal
field remains in \(\mathcal{A}\). If \(K\) acts as in Theorem 21, the
list of spanning fields is \(K\)-invariant. A finite \(K\)-invariant
span of eigenfunctions, closed under directional derivatives, is a
parallel normal subbundle, and \(\nabla\mathrm{II}=0\).

One-step \(R=0\) gives only \(\mathcal{V}\cdot\mathcal{V}\subset\mathcal{V}'\).
The \(3\)-jet uses \(\mathcal{V}'\cdot\mathcal{V}\subset\mathcal{A}\),
which is the tower and is not implied by a single vanishing tail.

A tower of height \(2\) suffices for the third jet of a window
supported in \(\mathcal{V}_0\): \(\mathcal{V}_0\cdot\mathcal{V}_0\subset\mathcal{V}_1\)
puts \(\Gamma(F,F)\) in \(\mathcal{V}_1\), and \(Z\cdot\Gamma(F,F)\) is a
derivative of that slot, not a product \(\mathcal{V}_1\cdot\mathcal{V}_1\).
If \(\iota\) also uses \(\mathcal{V}_1\), close the mixed products
\(\mathcal{V}_0\cdot\mathcal{V}_1\subset\mathcal{V}_1\) (or \(\mathcal{V}_2\)).
Stabilisation at \(\mathcal{V}_1\) is the two-step exact algebra.
One-step \(R=0\) without \(\mathcal{V}_1\) in the embedding is
exactly Theorem~3 when \(\dim\mathcal{V}_0=n+1\).

\subsection{The Torus Packet}
The first window of a sphere is not the only exact finite packet. On a compact flat manifold the characters of the translation lattice multiply by addition of frequencies, so any finite spanning set of frequencies is exact at a finite cutoff. Theorem 1 reconstructs the flat metric. Theorem 21 supplies transitivity from the group law rather than from Lemma 26.

Let $X=\mathbb{R}^n/\Gamma$ be a compact flat manifold, with $\Gamma$ a Bieberbach group, and write $\Lambda_{\mathrm{tr}}$ for the translation lattice of $\Gamma$. Let $\Lambda_{\mathrm{tr}}^*$ be its dual. Characters
$$e_\xi(x)=\mathrm{e}^{2\pi i\langle\xi,x\rangle},\qquad\xi\in\Lambda_{\mathrm{tr}}^*,$$
are eigenfunctions of $P=-\Delta$ with eigenvalues $\lambda_\xi=4\pi^2\lvert\xi\rvert^2$. They are holonomy-invariant when descended to $X$. Fix a finite set $S\subset\Lambda_{\mathrm{tr}}^*$ that spans $\Lambda_{\mathrm{tr}}^*\otimes\mathbb{R}$ as a real vector space, and let $\mathcal{V}$ be the real span of $\{1\}\cup\{\operatorname{Re}e_\xi,\operatorname{Im}e_\xi:\xi\in S\}$. Set
$$\Lambda'=4\pi^2\max_{\xi,\eta\in S}\lvert\xi+\eta\rvert^2.$$

\begin{proposition}With this packet, $R=0$ and W1–W2 hold. Theorem 1 identifies $(X,g)$ with the compact flat manifold whose dual is generated by $S$. The identity component of the isometry group in Theorem 21 contains the translation torus of the cover $\mathbb{R}^n/\Lambda_{\mathrm{tr}}$, and $X$ is a principal orbit of that torus modulo the holonomy of $\Gamma$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}The product of characters is a character, $e_\xi e_\eta=e_{\xi+\eta}$. If $\xi,\eta\in S$ then $\lvert\xi+\eta\rvert^2\le\max_{\xi',\eta'\in S}\lvert\xi'+\eta'\rvert^2$, so $\lambda_{\xi+\eta}\le\Lambda'$ and $e_\xi e_\eta\in\mathcal{V}'$. Real and imaginary parts are linear combinations of characters, so the same bound holds for products in $\mathcal{V}$. Thus $R=0$.

Evaluation $\iota:X\to\mathcal{V}^*$ is the composition of the covering $\mathbb{R}^n\to X$ with the map
$$x\mapsto\bigl(\cos 2\pi\langle\xi,x\rangle,\,\sin 2\pi\langle\xi,x\rangle\bigr)_{\xi\in S}.$$
The differential has full rank $n$ at every point because $S$ spans. If $S$ generates $\Lambda_{\mathrm{tr}}^*$ as a group then the kernel of $\iota$ on $\mathbb{R}^n$ is exactly $\Lambda_{\mathrm{tr}}$, so $\iota$ descends to an embedding of the translation torus, and hence of its holonomy quotient $X$. That is W1.

The cometric on characters is
$$\Gamma(e_\xi,e_\eta)=4\pi^2\langle\xi,\eta\rangle\,e_{\xi-\eta}$$
(up to conjugation). Restricted to $\mathcal{V}$ it has rank $n$ because $S$ spans. That is W2.

Theorem 1 therefore identifies $X$ with the character space of $(\mathcal{V},m)$. That space is the compact abelian group
$$\operatorname{Hom}(\langle S\rangle_{\mathbb{Z}},S^1)\cong\mathbb{R}^n/\Lambda_S,$$
where $\Lambda_S$ is the annihilator of the $\mathbb{Z}$-span of $S$. If $S$ generates $\Lambda_{\mathrm{tr}}^*$ then $\Lambda_S=\Lambda_{\mathrm{tr}}$ and the identification is a translation of the covering torus, descended to $X$. Lemma 14 recovers
$$g^{-1}(de_\xi,de_\eta)=\Gamma(e_\xi,e_\eta),$$
which is the flat metric with dual lattice $\Lambda_{\mathrm{tr}}^*$. Lemma 15 gives $\alpha$ constant.
The translation torus of $\mathbb{R}^n/\Lambda_{\mathrm{tr}}$ acts by isometries and preserves each character, hence preserves $\mathcal{V}$ and $\Phi$. It is a compact connected Lie subgroup of the group $K$ of Theorem 21. Its orbits are the fibres of the holonomy covering; on $X$ they descend to a single principal orbit because the holonomy is finite and acts freely. Lemma 25 is then the orbit decomposition of a Bieberbach manifold: $X$ is a compact quotient of a torus by a finite free linear action. Transitivity of the identity component on the cover is the group law of the character variety, not the spherical criterion of Lemma 26.
\end{proof}
\begin{remark} The dimension of $\mathcal{V}$ is $1+2\lvert S\rvert$ after passing to a real basis, and equals $n+1$ only in degenerate cases that do not occur for $n\ge 2$ with a single frequency length. Theorems 3–4 and Lemma 26 are therefore not available. The quadratic $Q=\sum u_a^2$ is a trigonometric polynomial of frequency at most $2\max_{\xi\in S}\lvert\xi\rvert$, not a constant. The torus packet is exact without being spherical.
\end{remark}
\begin{remark}A generic metric on the underlying torus has no exact finite window: products of first modes spray over infinitely many slots. Exactness of this packet is a certificate of flatness. If $S$ generates a proper sublattice, Theorem 1 reconstructs the coarser torus $\mathbb{R}^n/\Lambda_S$; the original manifold is a finite quotient of a finite cover of that torus.
\end{remark}
This subsection does not use Lemmas 27–28. The torus tower $\mathcal{V}_k=\operatorname{span}\{e_\xi:\lvert\xi\rvert\le kR\}$ is infinite and graded by $\Lambda_{\mathrm{tr}}^*$, which is the abelian case of the second clause of Lemma 28.

